<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chapultepec Castle</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chapultepec Castle stands on a hill in Chapultepec Park in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>. Begun in 1785 as a summer residence for the viceroys of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>, it later housed the military academy, whose young cadets died defending it against American troops in 1847. Maximilian I and Carlota rebuilt it as their imperial palace, and it later served as a presidential residence before becoming the National Museum of History in 1939.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The castle stands at the entrance to Chapultepec Park, at a height of 2,325 metres above sea level. Chapultepec is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a> word chapoltepēc, meaning on the hill of the grasshopper, and the hill was a sacred place for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a>. In August 1785 Viceroy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernardo-de-galvez">Bernardo de Gálvez</a> ordered a stately home built for himself at its highest point, to a plan by the engineer Francisco Bambitelli. Captain Manuel Agustín Mascaró took over the work, and was later accused of building a fortress from which to rebel against the Spanish Crown.</p><p>Gálvez died suddenly on 8 November 1786, which fuelled speculation that he was poisoned, though no evidence supports it. The Crown ordered the unfinished building auctioned, but no buyer came, and a later plan to house the General Archive of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> was not carried out. In 1803 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-von-humboldt">Alexander von Humboldt</a> condemned the sale of its windows to raise money, and in 1806 the municipal government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> bought it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The building was abandoned during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-war-of-independence">Mexican War of Independence</a> and for years afterwards. It became the Military College in 1833, and on 13 September 1847 the Niños Héroes died defending it when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> forces took it in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-american-war">Mexican-American War</a>. A mural above the main entrance honours them. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-marine-corps">United States Marine Corps</a> remembers the battle in the first line of its hymn, From the Halls of Montezuma, though the castle was built long after Montezuma's time.</p><p>When Maximilian von Hapsburg came to establish the Second Mexican Empire, the castle, then called Castillo de Miravalle, became the residence of the emperor and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlota-of-mexico">Empress Carlota</a> in 1864. European and Mexican architects renovated it in a neoclassical style, a roof garden was made, and Maximilian had a straight boulevard laid to the city centre, which after the republic's return in 1867 became the Paseo de la Reforma. An observatory opened there in 1878 but lasted five years. In 1882 President Manuel González made the castle the presidential residence, and most later presidents lived there until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazaro-cardenas">Lázaro Cárdenas</a> moved to Los Pinos in 1934.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In February 1939 Cárdenas established the castle as the National Museum of History, holding the collections of the former National Museum of Archaeology, History and Ethnography. The museum opened on 27 September 1944. With Iturbide Palace, it is one of only two royal palaces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> to have been inhabited by monarchs. Its design served as a model for the 13th Regiment Armory in Brooklyn, and it appears in films including Vera Cruz (1954) and Romeo + Juliet (1996).</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chapultepec Castle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The empire was financed by heavy loans, and the court at <b>Chapultepec Castle</b> was lavish.</blockquote><a href="/state/second-mexican-empire">Second Mexican Empire</a></li></ul></section>
